course: dictionary
lesson: 2
title: Animal words and context clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things in the world, and the sentence around them can show you what those things are like. «Tenrecs look like hedgehogs or shrews but are actually related to elephants and aardvarks, African animals that dig for ants.» The clue is «African animals that dig for ants», so an aardvark is an animal from Africa that digs for food.
explain: A name can also mislead you. The word aardvark comes from Afrikaans and means «earth pig», but it is not a pig. Read the whole sentence before you decide what a word means, and do not trust the sound or the name alone.
explain: Context can also show how a creature lives. «Warthogs often use old aardvark burrows and back in tail-first, so their tusks face the opening.» A burrow is a hole dug in the ground, so the sentence tells you that aardvarks dig burrows, and other animals can move into them later.
fact: An aardvark is a noun: an African mammal that digs into ant and termite nests.
fact: Aardvarks have a long snout, long ears, a thick tail and strong claws.
fact: An aardvark catches insects with its long, sticky tongue.
fact: Aardvarks live in «burrows» and come out mostly at night.
fact: The name aardvark comes from Afrikaans and means «earth pig», though it is not a pig.
exercise: «Warthogs often use old aardvark burrows.» What is a burrow? | A kind of food | *A hole dug in the ground | A tall tree | A loud noise
exercise: What does an aardvark use its long sticky tongue for? | To dig for water | To play music | To hang from trees | *To catch termites and ants
exercise: Why is the name aardvark a little misleading? | *It means «earth pig», but it is not a pig | It means «big pig», and it is one | It is a bird, not a mammal | It is a plant that grows in Africa
test: Which body part helps an aardvark dig into nests? | Wings | Whiskers | *Strong claws | Long ears
test: Where does an aardvark usually live, and when is it most active? | In a house, at dinner time | In trees, mostly in the day | In water, all day | *In burrows, mostly at night
exam: In «Tenrecs are actually related to elephants and aardvarks», what does related mean? | Painted in bright colours | Fighting against | Hidden under the ground | *Connected as a family or group, sharing ancestors
